---
// DETAILED DEEP CLEANING — `/deep-cleaning-san-jose/`. Rebuilt 2026-09-01 as the fourth
// gold-standard individual service page (docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20, §20A, §21), same
// approach as /hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/, /estate-cleanout-san-jose/ and
// /property-cleanouts-san-jose/: copy reconciled from
// docs/ASEPTACLEAN-SERVICE-LANDING-PAGE-SYSTEM.md §36 under §20A.1's order — landing copy → map
// to current route → check active/gated state → check claims, pricing and scope → check current
// copy authority → then implement.
//
//   01 SERVICE HERO             photograph + overlay, left-aligned, service confirmed on sight
//   02 WE DEFINE THE SCOPE      "Deep clean" can mean almost anything + routine-vs-detailed
//                               comparison, on a ruled two-column table rather than two cards
//   03 LOOK CLOSER              condition recognition by room, beside a large photograph — this
//                               page's signature movement in place of hoarding's document
//                               register / estate's save list, because this page has no
//                               five-category mechanism; the mechanism IS the written scope
//   04 SCOPE + EXCLUSIONS       what may be included, what is not automatic, cleaning vs damage,
//                               surface awareness — combined per §20A.6
//   05 HOW IT WORKS + PROOF     five-step field procedure + the honest no-photo-gallery gap
//   06 FIT + PRICE              basic-option reframe, two quiet columns, price drivers, no figure
//   07 QUESTIONS                seven, the ones that decide the purchase
//   08 CLOSE                    "Want the home cleaned in detail?"
//
// THE SPECIALIST THESIS IS §20A.5's, unchanged: **this is more work and more detail than normal
// housekeeping.** Every movement reinforces it without repeating the sentence — the hero states
// it, 02 explains why "deep clean" needs defining at all, 03 makes the condition visible, 04 is
// the written scope that makes the thesis binding rather than a slogan, 05 is the procedure and
// the honest evidence gap, 06 is where routine cleaning is the honest answer instead, and 07
// answers it as an objection ("is this the same as a normal deep clean?").
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES. The previous build: 1,082 words, one H1, SIXTEEN H2s, 4 H3s, zero
// photographs of actual cleaning subject matter (3 <img> on the page were nav/footer chrome),
// 81 links (mostly nav/footer). §20A.6 is explicit that the eleven conversion jobs are not
// eleven bands; this rebuild combines them into eight visual movements. The old page rendered
// through the generic `ServicePageLayout` + `doc27ServicePages.deepCleaningPage` record — the
// same "one template, swap the noun" pattern §20A.5 and §20A.25 Q14 rule out. That record's
// approved language (the checklist-is-the-product framing, the room-by-room quote logic) is
// mined for copy below, not discarded. Full accounting in docs/05-DECISIONS-LOG.md.
//
// ROUTE STATE IS UNCHANGED AND DELIBERATELY SO. Same slug, same canonical, same `index, follow`,
// same sitemap membership (confirmed indexable, gate: null, per doc27ServicePages.ts and
// AGENTS.md §2's route table). No route was created, renamed, redirected or re-indexed. The old
// internal "B10 checklist" gate note referenced in docs/05-DECISIONS-LOG.md was already reverted
// by owner correction before this rebuild — SITEMAP-MASTER always said `index` for this route,
// and the doc27 data record ships `gate: null`. Nothing here reopens that question.
//
// PRICING — AGENTS.md §4 IS ABSOLUTE. "No price figure is published anywhere on the site,"
// and §4 names the landing-page source's own "$1,350" sentence as the example. Doc 30 §20A.17
// repeats the check-before-publish instruction. There is no current owner decision reversing
// AGENTS.md §4, so no figure appears anywhere on this page — not in the hero, not near price
// logic, not in a footnote. Price logic is cost drivers + the $195 assessment framing only,
// exactly as hoarding, estate and property-cleanouts already ship.
//
// FOUNDER BACKGROUND — NOT RESTATED HERE. The landing-page source's "science, drug
// manufacturing, and hospital lab work" line does not match docs/21-CLAIMS-AND-COMPLIANCE-LAW.md
// §2.4's permitted background statements ("BS Biochemistry, UC Riverside · prior pharmaceutical
// manufacturing experience · prior histology and surgical pathology experience..."), and §2.4's
// verbatim founder-authority-limit clause is mandatory wherever that background appears. Rather
// than restate a credential claim on a service page — which risks reading as "this is why the
// cleaning is more thorough," an implied capability claim §2.1 does not allow — this page omits
// the background entirely and links to /about/, which already carries the full statement with
// its required limiting clause. The service argument stays condition + scope + labor, per the
// brief's own instruction not to lean on credentials alone.
//
// MOVE-IN INTENT — NOT A SEPARATE SECTION, NOT A NEW ROUTE. §20A.3: the landing-page source's
// move-in page is a future route candidate only. This page's fit section mentions an empty-home
// / pre-move scenario as one qualifying situation (it already exists as a `property_situation`
// option — "Already empty but requires detailed cleaning" — see AssessmentForm.astro), because
// that is real current intent this route already serves. No `/move-in-cleaning/` route, nav
// entry, or sitemap entry is added.
//
// NO CredentialBar ON THIS ROUTE. Same reasoning as the three prior rebuilds — both gated
// credential-bar claims (insurance wording, organic pathogen endorsement) are release-gated by
// AGENTS.md §3 pending a current COI match, and dropping a gated claim from an indexable page is
// the conservative direction. The component is untouched; only this call site omits it.
//
// NO EMBEDDED FORM. §20A.8's destination rule and §20A.9's "one assessment engine": CTAs link to
// /request-assessment/?service=deep-cleaning, which AssessmentForm.astro now recognizes (added
// alongside this page) and maps to the existing "Already empty but requires detailed cleaning"
// `property_situation` option — not a new one, and unclaimed by the other three service
// contexts, so the four service pages do not collide on the same pre-filled answer.
//
// IMAGES — AN HONEST PROOF GAP, STATED RATHER THAN FILLED WITH A MISMATCHED PHOTO. Every image
// currently in the repository (docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md §4) depicts room-scale clutter,
// neglect or an exterior — condition-development-hoarding-asurnipal.jpg,
// condition-development-neglected-kolforn.jpg, hero-development-residence-asurnipal.jpg and
// service-development-cleanup-asurnipal.jpg are already claimed by the hoarding, property-
// cleanouts and estate rebuilds; close-development-property-sanfranman59.jpg is an exterior
// street view already used as `/`'s close-CTA image. None depicts the kitchen-grease,
// shower-buildup, baseboard or window-track subject matter this page's argument actually needs,
// and the manifest caps atmosphere slots at three, "already knowingly exceeded by one," with an
// explicit "do not add a fifth without a new owner decision." A wrong-subject photo would also
// misrepresent the page's own argument (condition + detail, not clutter), and
// condition-development-reset-aismallard.jpg (the one empty-room frame) is barred from any
// before/after-adjacent role sitewide — on THIS page, whose whole argument is detail work made
// visible, that risk is sharper than on hoarding or estate, not weaker. So this page ships with
// NO PHOTOGRAPH AT ALL rather than force a mismatched asset into a detail-proof role. Movement
// 05 states the gap in the page's own voice, the same honesty move hoarding's proof section
// makes. Replace with real macro/detail photography before the Phase 0 owner shoot's successor
// pass — see docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md §5.
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { legal, site } from "@data/site";

const canonicalPath = "/deep-cleaning-san-jose/";

// REWRITTEN from the old doc27ServicePages.deepCleaningPage `seoTitle`/`metaDescription`, which
// described the old page's "one-time deep cleaning...quoted room by room" framing. This
// rebuild's H1 and thesis differ, so title/description are brought into agreement per §20A.22.
// Region corrected to "South Bay & Peninsula" — site.ts §B2's NAP wording of record — rather
// than the landing-page source's "Santa Clara County" (Atherton is San Mateo County).
const title = "Detailed Deep Cleaning in San Jose | Aseptaclean";
const description =
  "Detailed deep cleaning in San Jose and the South Bay & Peninsula, for homes that need more than routine housekeeping. The scope is written before the crew arrives, room by room.";

// §20A.8's destination rule + §20A.9's service branch. `?service=deep-cleaning` is read by
// AssessmentForm.astro (see that component's service-context block, added alongside this page).
const assessmentHref = "/request-assessment/?service=deep-cleaning";
const phoneHref = site.business.phoneUri;
const smsHref = site.business.smsUri;

// §36's routine-vs-detailed comparison, kept as two short columns rather than two cards.
const routine = ["Dust", "Vacuum", "Mop", "Wipe counters", "Basic bathroom surfaces", "Keeps the home maintained"] as const;

const detailed = [
  "Work through buildup",
  "Clean edges and trim",
  "Work on baseboards",
  "Clean doors and frames",
  "Detail fixtures",
  "Clean hard-to-reach areas",
  "Work through the kitchen in detail",
  "Work through bathrooms in detail"
] as const;

// §36's "LOOK CLOSER" room lists, kept as plain nouns — this is the condition-recognition
// movement, so the point is recognition speed, not full sentences.
const rooms = [
  {
    room: "Kitchen",
    items: ["grease on cabinet fronts", "dust and grease on cabinet tops", "buildup around the stove", "dirty appliance fronts", "backsplash buildup", "sink and faucet buildup", "edges and corners"]
  },
  {
    room: "Bathrooms",
    items: ["soap buildup", "shower glass", "tile surfaces", "fixtures", "sink buildup", "toilet areas", "baseboards", "edges and corners"]
  },
  {
    room: "Living areas & bedrooms",
    items: ["baseboards", "doors and frames", "trim", "switch plates", "reachable vents", "dust on ledges", "corners", "floor edges"]
  },
  {
    room: "Windows",
    items: ["interior glass", "sills", "tracks", "frames"],
    note: "when included in your scope"
  }
] as const;

// §36's "WHAT MAY BE PART OF YOUR CLEAN" — kept as a written-scope list, grouped by room.
const scope = [
  { area: "Kitchen", items: ["counters", "backsplash", "sink and fixtures", "stove surface", "appliance exteriors", "cabinet fronts", "cabinet tops where safe and reachable", "baseboards", "doors and frames", "floors"] },
  { area: "Bathrooms", items: ["shower surfaces", "tub", "shower glass", "sink", "fixtures", "toilet", "mirrors", "baseboards", "doors and frames", "floors"] },
  { area: "Living areas & bedrooms", items: ["reachable surfaces", "baseboards", "doors and frames", "trim", "switch plates", "reachable vents", "ledges", "floor edges", "floors"] }
] as const;

const scopeExtras = ["Interior windows", "Window tracks", "Inside the refrigerator", "Inside the oven", "Inside cabinets", "Other detail work agreed on before the job"] as const;

// §36's "WHAT IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY INCLUDED" — doc 30 §20A.13's "boundary language is a trust
// mechanism," kept as its own list rather than folded into the scope grid.
const notAutomatic = [
  "Inside every cabinet",
  "Inside every drawer",
  "Large amounts of interior glass",
  "Heavy oven buildup",
  "Heavy refrigerator cleaning",
  "Moving large furniture",
  "High areas that need special access",
  "Heavy buildup that needs more work",
  "Special surfaces that need a different process"
] as const;

// §36's cleaning-vs-damage list.
const cannotFix = ["Scratches", "Etching", "Worn finishes", "Burn marks", "Permanent stains", "Damaged stone", "Broken coatings", "Old damage"] as const;

const surfaces = ["Natural stone", "Porcelain", "Stainless steel", "Wood", "Painted cabinets", "Glass", "Brass", "Copper", "Tile"] as const;

// §36's five-step process, kept as a field procedure — §20A.14, not five cards.
const process = [
  {
    title: "Tell us about the home",
    detail: "Home size, bedrooms, bathrooms, condition, what you want cleaned, and when you need the work done. Photos help but are not required for the first call."
  },
  {
    title: "We look at the details",
    detail: "For larger or more detailed jobs, we may need photos or a walkthrough before giving a final price."
  },
  {
    title: "We set the scope, in writing",
    detail: "A clear list of the work included in the job. Extra work you want added goes in before the job starts, when possible."
  },
  {
    title: "We do the work",
    detail: "The crew works through the agreed scope — area by area, detail by detail."
  },
  {
    title: "We review the home against the scope",
    detail: "Before the job is closed out, the completed work is checked against what was written down, not against an adjective."
  }
] as const;

const goodFit = [
  "You want more than a basic cleaning",
  "The home has buildup",
  "Small details matter to you",
  "You want the scope clear before work starts",
  "You have not had a true deep clean in a long time",
  "You want a one-time reset",
  "You care more about the work than getting the cheapest quote"
] as const;

const poorFit = [
  "You only want the lowest price",
  "You need a quick two-hour clean",
  "You only need floors and counters cleaned",
  "You want repair or restoration work",
  "You expect cleaning to fix damaged surfaces",
  "The work is outside our current scope"
] as const;

// AGENTS.md §4 keeps the cost drivers and forbids the figure. §36's list.
const priceDrivers = [
  "home size",
  "number of bathrooms",
  "current condition",
  "amount of buildup",
  "number of windows",
  "appliances",
  "inside cabinets",
  "special surfaces",
  "high or hard-to-reach areas",
  "how much detail work you want"
] as const;

// §20A.18: 4–7 highest-value service-specific questions. Seven ship, chosen for effect on fit,
// scope and next step; the general ones (scheduling, payment) belong on /faq/.
const faq = [
  [
    "Is this the same as a normal deep clean?",
    [
      "Maybe not. The words “deep clean” can mean different things from one cleaning company to another.",
      "That is why we show you the written scope before the work starts, rather than asking you to trust an adjective."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "How long will the cleaning take?",
    [
      "It depends on the home. A detailed clean can take much longer than a basic cleaning.",
      "The size of the home is only one part of it — condition and scope matter too."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you clean inside cabinets?",
    [
      "Only when it is part of the scope.",
      "Inside cabinets can add a lot of time, so that work is priced before the job rather than assumed."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you clean ovens and refrigerators?",
    ["Yes, when they are included in the scope. Tell us if you want the inside cleaned."]
  ],
  [
    "Do you clean windows?",
    ["Interior window cleaning can be added to the scope. The number and type of windows affect the price."]
  ],
  [
    "Can you remove hard-water marks?",
    [
      "We can work on mineral buildup when the surface allows it, but not every mark can be cleaned away.",
      "Some glass or stone may already be etched or damaged. We will not promise a result the surface cannot give us."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "What if you find something that needs more work?",
    [
      "We tell you. We do not quietly add work and surprise you with a bigger bill.",
      "If the scope needs to change, we talk about it first."
    ]
  ]
] as const;

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Service",
    name: "Detailed Deep Cleaning in San Jose",
    serviceType: "Deep cleaning",
    provider: { "@id": new URL("/#business", site.urls.site).href },
    areaServed: { "@type": "City", name: "San Jose" }
  },
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.urls.home },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 2,
        name: "Detailed Cleaning",
        item: new URL("/detailed-cleaning/", site.urls.site).href
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 3,
        name: "Detailed Deep Cleaning",
        item: new URL(canonicalPath, site.urls.site).href
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map(([question, answer]) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: question,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer.join("\n\n") }
    }))
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} schema={schema}>
  {/* ══ 01 · SERVICE HERO ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.7: location context → unmistakable service H1 → specialist thesis → service CTA →
      phone as secondary. NO IMAGE — see the top-of-file note on the honest proof gap. Flat navy
      field rather than a photographic hero, so the absence of a photograph reads as a
      deliberate, restrained choice rather than a broken layout. */}
  <section class="dc-hero" aria-labelledby="dc-title">
    <div class="dc-hero__shell">
      <div class="dc-hero__content">
        <p class="dc-hero__eyebrow">
          Detailed Deep Cleaning · San Jose · {site.location.serviceArea}
        </p>
        <h1 id="dc-title" class="ac-type-h1-hub">Detailed Deep Cleaning</h1>
        <p class="dc-hero__lede">
          For homes that need more than a normal cleaning.
        </p>
        <p class="dc-hero__thesis">This is more work and more detail than normal housekeeping.</p>
        <p class="dc-hero__detail">
          Grease. Buildup. Baseboards. Trim. Fixtures. Tracks. Edges. Hard-to-reach areas.
        </p>

        <div class="dc-hero__actions">
          <a class="dc-btn dc-btn--light" href={assessmentHref}>Request a Deep Clean Estimate</a>
          {/* Suppression rule (AGENTS.md §3): never render an empty phone link. */}
          {phoneHref && (
            <a class="dc-hero__call" href={phoneHref}>Call {site.business.phone}</a>
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 02 · WE DEFINE THE SCOPE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.6's movement 02. The reframe leads: "deep clean" is a term every competitor uses
      differently, so the first job is telling the visitor what Aseptaclean means by it. The
      routine-vs-detailed comparison is a ruled two-column list, not two rounded cards
      (§20A.12: explain the basic option fairly, do not insult it). */}
  <section class="dc-define" aria-labelledby="define-title">
    <div class="dc-define__inner">
      <p class="dc-label">What we mean by it</p>
      <h2 id="define-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
        “Deep clean” can mean almost anything.
      </h2>
      <p class="dc-lede">
        One company may call a longer house cleaning a deep clean. Another may clean baseboards,
        tracks, doors, fixtures and buildup. That makes it hard to compare quotes — so we tell
        you what we mean before the job starts.
      </p>
      <p class="dc-statement">We set the scope before the job starts.</p>
      <p>
        You know what we are cleaning. You know what is not included. You know what you are
        paying for.
      </p>

      <div class="dc-compare">
        <div class="dc-compare__col">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Routine cleaning</h3>
          <p class="dc-compare__note">Keeps a home maintained. Often the right service.</p>
          <ul class="dc-rule-list">
            {routine.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="dc-compare__col dc-compare__col--detailed">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Aseptaclean detailed deep cleaning</h3>
          <p class="dc-compare__note">For the work that takes more time — the things that build up and the things that get skipped.</p>
          <ul class="dc-rule-list">
            {detailed.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="dc-statement dc-statement--wide">More detail takes more time. That is the difference.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 03 · LOOK CLOSER ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.6's condition-recognition movement, this page's signature composition in place of a
      document register — the mechanism on this page IS the written scope in movement 04, so this
      movement's job is purely recognition speed: can the visitor see their own house in this
      list within seconds? Room lists as open columns, not four equal cards. */}
  <section class="dc-recognize" aria-labelledby="recognize-title">
    <div class="dc-recognize__inner">
      <p class="dc-label">Look closer</p>
      <h2 id="recognize-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
        The home can look clean and still need detail work.
      </h2>
      <p class="dc-lede">
        Walk into a room and it may look fine. Look closer and you may see dust along the trim,
        grease above the kitchen, buildup around fixtures, dirty tracks, or dirt along the floor
        edges. That is the level we are looking at — not just whether the floor was vacuumed.
      </p>

      <div class="dc-rooms">
        {rooms.map((entry) => (
          <div class="dc-rooms__col">
            <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">
              {entry.room}
              {"note" in entry && entry.note && <span class="dc-rooms__note"> — {entry.note}</span>}
            </h3>
            <ul class="dc-inline-list">
              {entry.items.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="dc-statement">
        We do not race through the house. Good detail work takes time, which is why a detailed
        clean costs more than a normal cleaning.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 04 · SCOPE + EXCLUSIONS + CLEANING VS DAMAGE + SURFACES ═══════════════════════════
      §20A.6 combines what we do + what we do not + method into one movement. §20A.13: scope and
      exclusions are conversion content, not something to soften. Cleaning-vs-damage and
      surface-awareness both belong here because they answer the same underlying question — what
      can and cannot change about this surface today. */}
  <section class="dc-scope" aria-labelledby="scope-title">
    <div class="dc-scope__inner">
      <p class="dc-label">The written scope</p>
      <h2 id="scope-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">We build the scope around the home.</h2>
      <p class="dc-lede">
        Not every home needs the same work. Maybe the kitchen needs the most time. Maybe the
        shower has heavy buildup. Maybe the trim and small details were missed. We look at the
        condition first, then build the scope.
      </p>

      <div class="dc-scope__grid">
        {scope.map((group) => (
          <div class="dc-scope__col">
            <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">{group.area}</h3>
            <ul class="dc-rule-list dc-rule-list--tight">
              {group.items.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="dc-scope__extras">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Other work, when added to the scope</h3>
        <ul class="dc-inline-list">
          {scopeExtras.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="dc-notauto">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">What is not automatically included</h3>
        <p>Some work can add a lot of time. For example:</p>
        <ul class="dc-minus-list">
          {notAutomatic.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="dc-statement dc-statement--wide">
          We would rather price that work first than surprise you later.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="dc-scope__pair">
        <div class="dc-note">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Cleaning and damage are not the same thing.</h3>
          <p>Cleaning may remove dirt, grease, dust, soap and some types of buildup. Cleaning cannot fix:</p>
          <ul class="dc-inline-list">
            {cannotFix.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="dc-statement">
            More scrubbing is not always the answer. If we think the problem is damage and not
            dirt, we will tell you rather than keep scrubbing to make it look like we tried harder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="dc-note">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Different surfaces need different care.</h3>
          <p>Your home may have:</p>
          <ul class="dc-inline-list">
            {surfaces.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p>
            The same cleaner should not be used on every surface. Before working on a surface, we
            look at what it is and what condition it is in. If we are not sure a method is right
            for the surface, we do not guess.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="dc-disclaimer">{legal.scopeDisclaimer}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 05 · HOW IT WORKS + WHAT PROOF EXISTS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.14: vertical numbered sequence, not five cards. §20A.15 asks for before/during/after
      evidence; there is none to show honestly (see the top-of-file image note), so the gap is
      stated in the page's own voice rather than filled with a mismatched photograph —
      §20A.25 Q9's "real evidence or an honest proof gap" and AGENTS.md §0.3's "empty beats
      fake" both point the same way. */}
  <section class="dc-process" aria-labelledby="process-title">
    <div class="dc-process__inner">
      <p class="dc-label">How it works</p>
      <h2 id="process-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">Five steps, from first call to closeout.</h2>

      <ol class="dc-steps">
        {process.map((step, index) => (
          <li class="dc-step">
            <span class="dc-step__index" aria-hidden="true">
              {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <div class="dc-step__body">
              <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">{step.title}</h3>
              <p>{step.detail}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <div class="dc-proof">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-feature">What we can show you today.</h3>
        <p>
